<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#fief">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fief</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as feudum, fee, fiefdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A fief (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> feudum) was a central element of medieval contracts under feudal law: property or rights granted by an overlord to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, who held it in fealty in return for allegiance, services or payments. It was usually land or revenue-producing property, but could also be an office, hunting or fishing rights, a trade monopoly or a tax farm. The term feudum, first attested in 984, replaced the older beneficium, and by the middle of the 10th century fiefs had largely become hereditary, an heir doing homage and paying a relief. By the 12th century English and French kings and barons commuted military service for cash payments (scutages); feudal fiefs still exist by law in Guernsey.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishops in turn granted the valleys as a <b>fief</b> to a local lineage, the lords of Caboet, whose rights passed by marriage to the viscounts of Castellbò and, in the early thirteenth century, to the counts of Foix.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts of Hainaut were princes of the Holy Roman Empire, but from 1071 Countess Richilde held the county as a <b>fief</b> of the Bishop of Liège, an arrangement that persisted for centuries alongside imperial overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The county was an immediate territory of the Holy Roman Empire, its core lands around Hechingen held under the emperor, while other possessions were held as <b>fiefs</b> from neighbouring princes, notably the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was marked by partible inheritance among the nobility, much land held as free allods rather than <b>fiefs</b>, and a strong urban patriciate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The dukes also held the Duchy of Bar, united with Lorraine under René II after 1480, although the western Barrois beyond the Meuse had been held in <b>fief</b> of the French crown since 1301, which gave French kings a legal foothold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The investiture turned a personal lordship into a hereditary imperial <b>fief</b> and gave the Visconti a legitimacy that the communal traditions of Lombard cities had long denied them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
